course: biology
lesson: 253
title: Plants deeper: the oldest, tallest and strangest trees
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Stand at the foot of a coast redwood in California and the people beside you look like ants: the tallest tree known, Hyperion, is about 116 metres, taller than the Statue of Liberty with its base.
explain: Lift a log from the woodpile and you can count its age: each year a tree adds one ring of wood, pale in spring and darker in late summer.
explain: The oldest trees are often not the biggest but the toughest: twisted, half-dead bristlecone pines on cold dry mountains have lived for nearly 5,000 years.
explain: A tree has no pump, yet water climbs a hundred metres: leaves lose water into the air and pull up a thin chain of water from the roots, like sucking on a very long straw.
explain: Some trees are strange in other ways: a baobab stores water in a fat trunk, a banyan grows a forest of trunks, and some trees were known only as fossils until living ones were found.
fact: Coast redwoods in California are the tallest trees in the world; the tallest, Hyperion, is about 116 metres, its exact place is kept secret to protect it, and the fog they grow in gives water to their high leaves.
fact: Giant sequoias are the biggest trees by volume, General Sherman weighs about as much as ten blue whales or more, and their thick spongy bark resists fire, which helps their cones open.
fact: People once cut tunnels through giant sequoias for cars as a tourist trick, and one tunnel tree fell in a storm in 2017.
fact: Bristlecone pines in the White Mountains of California can live nearly 5,000 years; one named Methuselah is over 4,800 years old, already growing when the first Egyptian pyramids were built.
fact: A tree adds one ring of wood each year, wide rings mean good wet years and thin rings dry or cold ones, and scientists use rings in old timbers to date buildings and learn about past weather.
fact: Water rises in a tree because leaves lose water into the air and pull up a chain of water through thin tubes; water sticks together like a rope, and scientists think trees cannot grow much taller than about 120 to 130 metres.
fact: Baobabs store thousands of litres of water in their huge spongy trunks and drop their leaves in the dry season; some hollow baobabs have been used as a shop, a prison and even a pub.
fact: Some British yews may be 2,000 years old or more, like the Fortingall Yew in Scotland, but their age is hard to measure because they grow new trunks inside hollow old ones; almost all of a yew is poisonous except the red flesh of the berry.
fact: A banyan drops roots from its branches that become new trunks, and the Great Banyan in Kolkata covers an area like a big park with thousands of root-trunks, though its main trunk was removed in 1925.
fact: Bonsai are ordinary trees kept small by careful cutting of roots and branches in small pots, not special tiny kinds, and some bonsai are hundreds of years old.
fact: The dawn redwood was known only from fossils until living trees were found in China in the 1940s; seeds were sent round the world and it now grows in many parks.
fact: The Wollemi pine was found in a hidden canyon in Australia in 1994, with fewer than 100 wild trees, and firefighters were reported to have saved them from the bushfires of 2019 and 2020; garden-centre trees help protect the wild ones, because people can grow one at home instead of hunting for the secret wild trees.
fact: Mountain ash eucalyptus trees in Tasmania and Victoria are the tallest flowering plants, up to about 100 metres (redwoods are taller but carry cones, not flowers), and they need fire to make new seedlings.
exercise: Which are the tallest trees in the world? | Coast redwoods in California | Baobab trees on the plains of Africa | Oak trees in England | Palm trees in Egypt
exercise: Why is the exact place of the tree Hyperion kept secret? | To protect it from crowds of visitors | Because nobody has ever been able to find it again | Because it moves to a new spot every year | Because it grows on a private island far away
exercise: How do coast redwoods get water to their highest leaves in foggy places? | The fog gives water to the high leaves | They have a strong pump inside their trunk, like a heart | Birds carry water up | They do not need water up there
exercise: What are giant sequoias the biggest trees by? | Volume, the amount of wood | Height only, from root to tip | The number of leaves they carry | Age only, counted by their rings
exercise: Why does fire not kill most giant sequoias? | Their thick spongy bark resists fire | They grow with their roots under water | They are made of stone | Fire never reaches the forests of California at all
exercise: Why were tunnels cut through some giant sequoias? | As a tourist trick for cars to drive through | To let rivers and streams pass through the forest | To make homes for bears | To let more light into the forest floor for young trees
exercise: How old is the bristlecone pine called Methuselah? | Over 4,800 years | About 48 years old | About 480 years old | Over 48,000 years
exercise: How can you tell the age of a tree from a log? | Count the rings, one for each year | Measure how heavy it is | Count the branches | Look at the colour of the bark
exercise: What does a thin tree ring usually mean? | A dry or cold year | A very wet and rainy year | The tree was cut that year | The tree was a baby
exercise: What pulls water up to the top of a tall tree? | Leaves losing water into the air pull up a chain of water | A heart beating slowly deep in the trunk | The wind pushing it up from the bottom of the trunk to the top | Birds sucking it up
exercise: How does a baobab survive the long dry season? | It stores water in its huge spongy trunk | It drinks salty water from the sea through long roots | It sleeps under the ground | It borrows water from other trees
exercise: Why is it hard to say exactly how old an ancient yew is? | It grows new trunks inside hollow old ones | It has no wood at all, only soft bark and leaves | It changes its rings every year | Nobody is allowed to look at it
exercise: Which part of a yew is not poisonous? | The red flesh of the berry | The seed inside the berry | The leaves of the tree | The bark
exercise: How does one banyan tree look like a whole forest? | Roots drop from its branches and become new trunks | Many seeds fall down and grow up in a ring round it | People plant copies next to it | It splits in two every single year from top to bottom
exercise: How is a bonsai kept small? | By careful cutting of roots and branches in a small pot | By using a special tiny kind of tree that never grows tall | By giving it no water | By keeping it in the dark
exercise: Why is the dawn redwood called a living fossil? | It was known only from fossils before living trees were found | It is made of stone | It is millions of years old itself, the oldest living tree on Earth | It grows only in museums
exercise: How many wild Wollemi pines were found in 1994? | Fewer than 100 | Over a million of them | Exactly one, all alone | About 10,000 trees
exercise: Which are the tallest flowering plants? | Mountain ash eucalyptus trees | Sunflowers | Coast redwoods in California | Bamboo
test: In a film a talking willow tree says she is four hundred years old. Can trees really be that old? | Yes, and some, like bristlecone pines, are nearly 5,000 years old | No, no tree anywhere on Earth lives for more than about 100 years at most | Yes, but only willows | No, trees die after 40 years
test: «why don't trees just keep growing taller forever» What is the best answer? | Water cannot be pulled up high enough fast enough above about 120 to 130 metres | Birds land on the tops and break them | They hit the clouds | The wind cuts off the tops each year and they have to start again from the bottom
test: «my bonsai is a baby tree, special mini sort innit» What is right? | It is an ordinary tree kept small by cutting, it could grow big in the ground | Yes, bonsai are a special tiny kind of tree that grows in the wild in Japan | Yes, they come from a tiny forest | No, bonsai are plastic
test: «counted 23 rings on the log, no wait 32. how old was the tree» | About 32 years | About 23 years | About 55 years | About 9 years
test: «10 wide rings and 5 thin ones on my log. how many good wet years» | 10 | 5 | 15 | 50
test: What would happen if a scientist found a beam with rings in an old house and matched the pattern to dated trees? | She could tell roughly when the tree was cut | She could tell exactly who had built the house and when | She could tell what colour the house was | She could tell nothing at all
test: «that massive fat tree in Africa, there's a pub inside it?? no way» What is right? | Some hollow baobabs really have been used as a shop, a prison and a pub | It is a joke, because baobabs are thin | Pubs are only built in oak trees | Baobabs are full of water, so nobody could ever get inside one to drink
test: «saw a big tree in Kolkata, like 1000s of trunks, must be loads of trees» What is it? | The Great Banyan, one tree with roots that became trunks | A forest of different trees growing very close together | A bamboo field | A fake tree for tourists
test: A wildfire passes through a giant sequoia grove. What can happen next? | The trees survive and the heat helps their cones open | All the sequoias die at once in the flames and the smoke | The sequoias stop making seeds for many years | The trees grow tunnels
test: «yew berry looks nice, ok to eat the whole thing?» What is right? | No, the seed inside is very poisonous, leave yew berries alone | Yes, all of a yew is safe | Yes, but only the seed | Only if you cook it
test: Why do gardeners buying Wollemi pines help the wild ones? | Fewer people go looking for the secret wild trees | The wild trees need gardeners to come and water them every summer | The garden trees send their seeds back to the wild ones | It somehow makes the wild trees in the gorge grow faster
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the means by which water ascends a tall tree? | The evaporation of water from the leaves draws a continuous column of water upwards through narrow tubes | A muscular organ within the trunk propels the water upwards | The water is conveyed upwards by the action of the wind | The roots exert a pressure sufficient by itself to raise water to any height whatsoever, however tall the tree
exam: Identify the principal method by which the age of a felled tree may be determined. | The enumeration of the annual rings within its trunk | The measurement of its total weight with great precision | The enumeration of its branches | The examination of the colour of its bark
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dawn redwood. | It was known solely from fossils until living specimens were discovered in China in the 1940s | It has never been discovered in a living state, being known to science only from fossils in rocks | It was first planted by gardeners in the 1990s | It is the tallest tree presently living
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the precise location of the tree known as Hyperion is withheld from the public. | The protection of the tree from damage by numerous visitors | The tree being situated upon private property abroad, in a distant country | The tree having been felled many years ago | The location being unknown to scientists
